Hey y'all! Sorry for yet another late email! I'm gonna try (and probably
fail) to keep this one pretty short since daylight savings has thrown me
off and I'm pretty sleepy.

On Monday, I started my new assignment at the ELE preschool here in Spanish
Fork! It's a small preschool for kids in vulnerable homes, and it is a
*delight!
*As the resident New PersonTM in the building, I was instantly the center
of attention, and I spent the day swarmed by kids clambering for my
attention, clinging onto my arms, grabbing my earrings to admire them, and
attempting to climb onto my lap. As a missionary, I'm not allowed to have
kids on my lap, so I gently slid them off, but it was great getting to hold
their hands, play with them, and comfort them during tantrums, when at New
Hope our physical contact was limited to high-fives. Kids are absolutely
adorable, and I'm so happy to be back at an assignment where I can use my
gifts of working with kids to serve these precious ones in my community.

Monday evening, after a chaotic but joyful first day at the preschool, I
was invited to join the young single adult ward activity at Cornbelly's, a
massive corn maze attraction! I went with Aspen aka Sister Brinkerhoff and
some other wonderful friends, and we had a blast going on all the rides,
climbing around their ropes courses, and wandering through the maze! It was
definitely the most extravagant corn maze/pumpkin patch attraction I've
been to, although the maze itself was not as large and difficult as some
others I've been to.

On Tuesday, Wednesday, and Thursday, I worked on doing some food
photography for a neighborhood friend who has a special gluten-free bakery!
She brought over lots of treats, and I got to dust off my skills from my
photography classes in High School. It definitely isn't as good as I could
do with a proper camera, backdrop, and lighting setup, but I'm doing my
best to edit them up to be Instagram-worthy for her blog. :)

Friday was Halloween, my favorite holiday!! Most people like Christmas the
most, but I am absolutely captivated by Halloween's vibes. Cooler weather,
spooky stories, creative costumes, tasty goodies, falling leaves,
candlelights, migrating birds... everything about October tickles at my
love for all things forest and folklore related.

For Halloween, I dressed up as Wirt from the animated television series
Over the Garden Wall. It's the perfect October show as it captures
perfectly all of the elements of fall I just described. Serving at the
museum in said costume was absolutely whimsical. My uncle from Colorado
also came and visited, and I had a great time giving him a tour around the
museum! He loves Porter Rockwell, so we beelined for the Wild West room and
he seemed to enjoy our collection of artifacts related to the famous LDS
lawman.

Finally, today, I got the fantastic opportunity to witness dear little
Adalyn's baby blessing! She's so adorable, and I am so excited to watch her
grow up and become such a wonderfully strong, intelligent, and kind person,
just like her parents.

That ought to be all for this email, I've had a busy busy week and it's
about time I go to bed, but I'm so grateful for all the things I got to do
this week! I have such awesome friends and family, and every day reminds me
how blessed I am to be a service missionary where I can share love with all
the people around me.

______________________
Fun fact of the week: The main character of Over the Garden Wall, Wirt, was
voiced by Elijah Wood, who also played Frodo in the Lord of the Rings
movies.
_______________________

You all are fantastic! I hope you all have a great first week of November!
Much love,
-Sister Wessman
Utah Spanish Fork Mission

Photos aren't working so I'll either send them next week or, more likely,
forget completely and you'll never see them which is a real shame since
they're very good this week =F0=9F=A5=B2=F0=9F=91=8D

