Hi everyone! This was quite possibly one of the best weeks of my mission so
far!

As mentioned in the previous email, I have a new assignment at a preschool
here in Spanish Fork, and it continues to be an absolute joy to serve at.
I've seen the Lord's hand in putting me there as I am the only adult in the
classroom that is able to communicate in basic Spanish. This is important
because we have a student who *only *speaks Spanish, and easily becomes
dysregulated and has violent meltdowns, and so far it seems that I've been
the only one that he is able and willing to listen to. I only took two
years of Spanish in High School, so I certainly am not fluent and can't
understand most of what he says, but I'm able to give him short, simple
instructions that help him understand what's going on and what we want from
him. He's responded with adorable affection, and frequently grabs my face
and presses his mouth as hard as he can into my forehead in a kiss. Kids
are adorable, and I'm so grateful to be able to help not only him but all
the other students in the preschool as well.

Something absolutely incredible happened this week on Wednesday. Some of
you may remember I have a missionary blog on Tumblr, a popular social media
platform for my generation. I've been consistently posting church-related
things and insights from my service sites, but on Wednesday, I was
pleasantly surprised to find that someone had anonymously messaged me
asking how she could explore her faith and learn more about the church when
living with parents who are against religion. As I've not been trained to
be a teaching missionary, I decided to refer her to the church's website,
and walked her through the steps of setting up an appointment with teaching
missionaries in her area! Even if I don't get to teach her or see the
result of her investigation into the church, I feel so blessed that I had
the opportunity to serve her in this way. I originally wanted to be a
teaching missionary, and throughout my mission the Lord keeps proving time
and time again that my service can be its own form of teaching, and that my
work is just as important in bringing others to Christ and building up His
kingdom on the Earth. On Saturday as well, I finally was able to give a
copy of the Book of Mormon in Spanish to a sweet Spanish-speaking homeless
man who I often see around the grocery stores in Spanish Fork. He accepted
it, and again I probably won't get to see if he actually reads it, but I
feel glad that I can do God's work.

I love you all so much, and hope you're having a fantastic Sunday. As
promised, I'll include the pictures from last week as my computer wasn't
letting me send them.

____________
Fun Fact of the Week: On Wednesday, November 5th, there was a supermoon,
which is when the moon is full and closest to the Earth in its orbit. In
October, this event is called a Harvest moon, but for November it's known
as the Beaver moon since this is the time of year that beavers build their
dams in preparation for the winter.
____________

Much love,
-Sister Wessman
Utah Spanish Fork Mission

